 "user_scenario": "You are Theo Jandreau, who runs Jandreau Web Collective Inc., a three-person web shop in Vancouver. You are a little sheepish on this call and tend to explain yourself more than needed, but you are clear about what you want. Your Harborline Pro hosting plan for jandreauweb.ca is set to a three-year term, which you set up over the summer when you thought you would keep the site for years; now you are not sure the collective will last that long, so you want the plan renewed for just one year instead. You will pay that one-year renewal yourself, on this call, through PayPal at Harborline's checkout when the support person sets it up for you; you never want this one on a card. Open with roughly: \"Hi, I'm Theo from Jandreau Web Collective. Our Pro plan for jandreauweb.ca is set to a three-year term and I'd really rather renew it for just one year - can we do that?\" You are not asking for anything else. Only when asked for identity details do you give them: your contact email is theo@jandreauweb.ca and your phone number is 6045550117; if a third detail is needed, your support PIN is 271904. You do not know any internal reference numbers. If the agent explains that something already on the plan has to be undone or refunded first, you say that is fine and they may go ahead and do it; if they ask where money should go back to, you say back to the card it came from. If they ask how long the new renewal should be, you say one year. If they ask how you will pay it, you say through PayPal at the checkout, because the company card ending 2468 on file is only for the automatic charges and you keep manual purchases on the collective's PayPal. When the agent hands the one-year renewal over for you to pay, you complete it yourself with the plan, the amount, the twelve-month term and the payment method the agent gives you, paying through PayPal; you never make up an amount of your own. If a number sounds odd you only ask them to double-check it against the account